    <span class="badge featured">Field Note</span>
    <h1>Lineage you can audit, without a catalogue</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">Delta transaction history, notebook-level logging and <code>semantic-link</code> give you a queryable evidence trail from source file to published measure &mdash; built into the medallion architecture rather than bolted onto it. Here's the implementation, and where it stops being enough.</p>
    <div class="article-meta">
      <span>Zohal Zahir</span><span>&bull;</span>
      <time datetime="2026-08-03">August 2026</time><span>&bull;</span>
      <span>11 min read</span><span>&bull;</span>
      <span>Microsoft Fabric</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<article class="article-body">
  <div class="container">

    <p>Most governance conversations in Fabric end at the same place: buy Purview. Which is a reasonable answer for an organisation with a data governance function, and a non-answer for the ones I mostly work with &mdash; multi-site operators with somewhere between eight and fifty locations, a compliance obligation that is very real, and no appetite for a second platform to administer.</p>

    <p>So the question I've been working on is narrower: <strong>how much defensible lineage can you build from the pipeline itself?</strong> Not a catalogue. An evidence trail that falls out of the platform running normally, and that survives someone asking where a number came from eighteen months later.</p>

    <p>The answer turns out to be: quite a lot, if you treat the audit tables as first-class outputs rather than debug logging. This is the implementation I've settled on, and the honest boundaries of it.</p>

    <div class="pull">
      <p>Debug logging tells you why last night's run failed. An audit trail tells you why a number in a board pack from March is what it is. They are not the same artefact, and the difference is mostly discipline.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>The design constraint that shapes everything</h2>

    <p>One rule, and it dictates most of what follows: <strong>the audit layer never contains data values.</strong> Row counts, hashes, timestamps, rule outcomes, column names. Never a sample row, never a rejected record's contents, never an exception message that might carry a field value.</p>

    <p>This is easy to violate accidentally. A well-meaning <code>except Exception as e: log(str(e))</code> will happily write a customer name into your audit table the first time a constraint fails on a bad row. In a health or care context that turns your compliance artefact into a breach surface, and the audit table is usually the one with the loosest access control because nobody thinks of it as sensitive.</p>

    <p>Everything below assumes that constraint.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left:3px solid var(--copper)">
      <h4>See it running</h4>
      <p>Everything below is surfaced in a report the client operates themselves. <a href="demo-lineage.html" class="inline"><strong>Open the interactive demo &rarr;</strong></a> &mdash; trace a measure back to its source file, see quality per column at each layer, and watch a blocking rule hold a figure back. Synthetic data throughout.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Bronze: what Delta already gives you, and what it doesn't</h2>

    <p>Delta Lake hands you a substantial amount of lineage for free, and it's underused. Every write to a Delta table produces a transaction log entry, queryable directly:</p>

    <p class="code-label">Notebook — Bronze</p>
    <pre><code><span class="c"># Delta transaction history is already an audit trail.</span>
<span class="c"># Every version, operation, timestamp and row count, at no cost.</span>
history_df = spark.sql(<span class="s">"DESCRIBE HISTORY bronze.incident_register"</span>)

history_df.select(
    <span class="s">"version"</span>, <span class="s">"timestamp"</span>, <span class="s">"operation"</span>,
    <span class="s">"operationMetrics.numOutputRows"</span>,
    <span class="s">"operationParameters"</span>
).show(truncate=<span class="k">False</span>)</code></pre>

    <p>That gives you version, operation type, wall-clock time and rows written, per table, retained according to your log retention settings. Combined with time travel, you can reconstruct exactly what a table contained at a point in the past:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="c"># What did Bronze look like when that board pack was produced?</span>
as_at = spark.read.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>) \
    .option(<span class="s">"timestampAsOf"</span>, <span class="s">"2026-03-31 23:59:59"</span>) \
    .table(<span class="s">"bronze.incident_register"</span>)</code></pre>

    <p><strong>What Delta history does not tell you</strong> is the part that matters for an audit: which source system the file came from, when the <em>source</em> last changed, what the extract window was, whether the payload you processed is byte-identical to the one you received, and what triggered the run. None of that is inferable from the transaction log. So Bronze needs an explicit control table written before the data lands.</p>

    <p class="code-label">Notebook — Bronze ingestion control</p>
    <pre><code><span class="k">import</span> hashlib, uuid, json
<span class="k">from</span> datetime <span class="k">import</span> datetime, timezone
<span class="k">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class="k">import</span> Row

<span class="k">def</span> sha256_of(path: str) -> str:
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    <span class="k">with</span> open(path, <span class="s">"rb"</span>) <span class="k">as</span> f:
        <span class="k">for</span> chunk <span class="k">in</span> iter(<span class="k">lambda</span>: f.read(1 &lt;&lt; 20), <span class="k">b""</span>):
            h.update(chunk)
    <span class="k">return</span> h.hexdigest()

load_id    = str(uuid.uuid4())
started    = datetime.now(timezone.utc)
src_path   = <span class="s">"Files/landing/incident_register_20260701.csv"</span>

<span class="k">try</span>:
    df = spark.read.option(<span class="s">"header"</span>, <span class="k">True</span>).csv(src_path)
    rows = df.count()
    df.write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>) \
      .option(<span class="s">"mergeSchema"</span>, <span class="s">"false"</span>) \
      .saveAsTable(<span class="s">"bronze.incident_register"</span>)
    status, failure = <span class="s">"Success"</span>, <span class="k">None</span>

<span class="k">except</span> Exception <span class="k">as</span> e:
    rows, status = 0, <span class="s">"Failed"</span>
    <span class="c"># Exception TYPE only. The message may contain field values.</span>
    failure = type(e).__name__

spark.createDataFrame([Row(
    load_id            = load_id,
    source_system      = <span class="s">"IncidentRegister"</span>,
    source_object      = src_path.split(<span class="s">"/"</span>)[-1],
    source_modified_utc= <span class="k">None</span>,          <span class="c"># populate from mtime / API header</span>
    extract_window_start = <span class="s">"2026-04-01"</span>,
    extract_window_end   = <span class="s">"2026-06-30"</span>,
    rows_received      = rows,
    file_hash          = sha256_of(src_path),
    triggered_by       = notebookutils.runtime.context.get(<span class="s">"activityId"</span>),
    load_started_utc   = started,
    load_completed_utc = datetime.now(timezone.utc),
    status             = status,
    failure_detail     = failure,
)]).write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>).saveAsTable(<span class="s">"audit.ingestion"</span>)</code></pre>

    <div class="callout">
      <h4>Why the hash earns its place</h4>
      <p>Of everything in that table, <strong>file_hash</strong> is the column that does the most work in an actual audit. It lets you demonstrate that the payload you processed is the payload you were given, unaltered. It also catches the silent duplicate re-load &mdash; same hash, different load_id, same extract window &mdash; which is otherwise very hard to spot after the fact.</p>
      <p>Note the failure path writes <code>type(e).__name__</code> rather than <code>str(e)</code>. That is deliberate, and it's the single most common place this design leaks data.</p>
    </div>

    <p>One more Bronze rule: <strong>append only, and never <code>mergeSchema</code> silently.</strong> If the source added a column, that is a fact worth failing on and recording, not something to absorb quietly. Schema drift discovered six months later during a reconciliation is expensive; schema drift that fails a load at 2am is cheap.</p>

    <h2>Silver: rows in, rows out, and where PII gets decided</h2>

    <p>Silver is where transformation happens, and where most of the useful audit signal is generated. The single most valuable thing you can log here is boring: <strong>rows in versus rows out versus rows rejected, per rule.</strong></p>

    <p>I have lost count of the number of investigations that collapsed to a five-minute lookup because this existed. A figure looks wrong; you check the transformation log; 4,102 rows went in, 3,847 came out, 255 were rejected on a missing facility code from one site that changed its export format. That's a conversation with an operations manager, not a two-day forensic exercise.</p>

    <p class="code-label">Notebook — Silver conform with rejection accounting</p>
    <pre><code><span class="k">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class="k">import</span> functions <span class="k">as</span> F

RULES = {
    <span class="s">"facility_code_present"</span>: F.col(<span class="s">"facility_code"</span>).isNotNull(),
    <span class="s">"incident_date_valid"</span>:   F.col(<span class="s">"incident_date"</span>).between(<span class="s">"2020-01-01"</span>, <span class="s">"2030-12-31"</span>),
    <span class="s">"severity_in_domain"</span>:    F.col(<span class="s">"severity"</span>).isin(<span class="s">"1"</span>, <span class="s">"2"</span>, <span class="s">"3"</span>, <span class="s">"4"</span>),
}

src = spark.table(<span class="s">"bronze.incident_register"</span>).filter(F.col(<span class="s">"load_id"</span>) == load_id)
rows_in = src.count()

<span class="c"># Tag each row with the first rule it fails — counts only, never values.</span>
tagged = src
<span class="k">for</span> name, cond <span class="k">in</span> RULES.items():
    tagged = tagged.withColumn(<span class="s">f"_fail_{name}"</span>, (~cond).cast(<span class="s">"int"</span>))

fail_cols = [c <span class="k">for</span> c <span class="k">in</span> tagged.columns <span class="k">if</span> c.startswith(<span class="s">"_fail_"</span>)]
rejections = tagged.agg(*[F.sum(c).alias(c) <span class="k">for</span> c <span class="k">in</span> fail_cols]).collect()[0].asDict()

clean = tagged.filter(F.greatest(*[F.col(c) <span class="k">for</span> c <span class="k">in</span> fail_cols]) == 0).drop(*fail_cols)
rows_out = clean.count()

clean.write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>).saveAsTable(<span class="s">"silver.incident"</span>)

spark.createDataFrame([Row(
    run_id           = str(uuid.uuid4()),
    load_id          = load_id,
    notebook_name    = <span class="s">"nb_incident_conform"</span>,
    notebook_version = git_commit_sha,        <span class="c"># injected by the pipeline</span>
    input_tables     = <span class="s">"bronze.incident_register"</span>,
    output_table     = <span class="s">"silver.incident"</span>,
    rows_in          = rows_in,
    rows_out         = rows_out,
    rows_rejected    = rows_in - rows_out,
    rejection_detail = json.dumps(rejections),   <span class="c"># counts by rule</span>
    started_utc      = started,
    completed_utc    = datetime.now(timezone.utc),
)]).write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>).saveAsTable(<span class="s">"audit.transformation"</span>)</code></pre>

    <p>Two things worth noting in that pattern. <code>rejection_detail</code> holds counts per rule, not the rejected rows &mdash; if you need the rows for remediation, they belong in a quarantine table with the same access controls as the source, not in the audit schema. And <code>notebook_version</code> carries the git commit, which is what makes a number traceable to the exact code that produced it. If your pipeline can't inject that, it's worth the plumbing.</p>

    <h3>The PII decision table</h3>

    <p>Silver is also where you decide what personal information proceeds, and this is the table I'd argue is the most audit-relevant artefact in the whole design. One row per column that carries or derives from personal information:</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>source_column</code></td><td>Fully qualified source path</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>treatment</code></td><td>Dropped · Pseudonymised · Hashed · Carried</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>surrogate_table</code></td><td>Where the mapping lives, if pseudonymised</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>justification</code></td><td>Why it proceeds at all, in prose</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>approved_by</code> / <code>approved_date</code></td><td>Who made the call, and when</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>It's unglamorous and it takes ten minutes to populate. It also answers, instantly, the question that otherwise takes a week to reconstruct: <em>what personal information reaches the reporting layer, and who decided it should?</em></p>

    <p>Where you pseudonymise, keep the surrogate mapping in a separate lakehouse with its own workspace permissions. If the reporting model can join back to the mapping, you haven't pseudonymised anything &mdash; you've added a step.</p>

    <h2>Semantic model: extracting definitions with semantic-link</h2>

    <p>The layer that generates the most disagreement is the one people document least. A measure's DAX is the actual definition of a business metric, and it changes without ceremony.</p>

    <p><code>semantic-link</code> lets you pull model metadata into a notebook, which means you can snapshot it on a schedule and keep a dated record of what every measure meant on any given day.</p>

    <p class="code-label">Notebook — model snapshot</p>
    <pre><code><span class="k">import</span> sempy.fabric <span class="k">as</span> fabric
<span class="k">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class="k">import</span> functions <span class="k">as</span> F

DATASET = <span class="s">"Care Quality Reporting"</span>
snapshot_date = datetime.now(timezone.utc).date()

measures      = fabric.list_measures(DATASET)
relationships = fabric.list_relationships(DATASET)
columns       = fabric.list_columns(DATASET)

<span class="c"># Measures: name, DAX, description, folder — the definitional record</span>
(spark.createDataFrame(measures)
   .withColumn(<span class="s">"snapshot_date"</span>, F.lit(snapshot_date))
   .write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>)
   .saveAsTable(<span class="s">"audit.model_measure_snapshot"</span>))

(spark.createDataFrame(relationships)
   .withColumn(<span class="s">"snapshot_date"</span>, F.lit(snapshot_date))
   .write.format(<span class="s">"delta"</span>).mode(<span class="s">"append"</span>)
   .saveAsTable(<span class="s">"audit.model_relationship_snapshot"</span>))</code></pre>

    <p>Once that's running, a genuinely useful query becomes available &mdash; measures whose definition changed between two dates:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="k">SELECT</span> a.measure_name,
       a.expression <span class="k">AS</span> before_dax,
       b.expression <span class="k">AS</span> after_dax
<span class="k">FROM</span>   audit.model_measure_snapshot a
<span class="k">JOIN</span>   audit.model_measure_snapshot b
       <span class="k">ON</span> a.measure_name = b.measure_name
<span class="k">WHERE</span>  a.snapshot_date = <span class="s">'2026-03-31'</span>
  <span class="k">AND</span>  b.snapshot_date = <span class="s">'2026-06-30'</span>
  <span class="k">AND</span>  a.expression &lt;&gt; b.expression;</code></pre>

    <p>That query has ended several arguments for me. "The number changed" is usually true, and usually because someone adjusted a filter three months ago with good intentions and no record.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <h4>On measure descriptions</h4>
      <p>The snapshot is only as useful as what's in the model. Every measure gets a description, and where a definition embeds a judgement, the description says so.</p>
      <p>A measure averaging only completed assessments should be named <code>Avg Risk Score (Completed Only)</code> and its description should state that it therefore understates true risk, because completion is not random. Naming a limitation in the model is worth more than a paragraph in a document nobody opens.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Wiring it together: the trace</h2>

    <p>Individually these tables are logs. Joined, they're a lineage graph:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="k">SELECT</span> m.measure_name,
       m.expression,
       t.output_table,
       t.notebook_name,
       t.notebook_version,
       t.rows_in, t.rows_out, t.rows_rejected,
       i.source_system,
       i.source_object,
       i.file_hash,
       i.load_completed_utc
<span class="k">FROM</span>   audit.model_measure_snapshot m
<span class="k">JOIN</span>   audit.transformation t <span class="k">ON</span> t.output_table = m.source_table
<span class="k">JOIN</span>   audit.ingestion     i <span class="k">ON</span> i.load_id      = t.load_id
<span class="k">WHERE</span>  m.snapshot_date = <span class="s">'2026-06-30'</span>
  <span class="k">AND</span>  m.measure_name  = <span class="s">'Incidents per 1,000 Bed Days'</span>;</code></pre>

    <p>One row, six hops: published measure &rarr; its DAX &rarr; the Gold table &rarr; the notebook and commit that built it &rarr; the Bronze load &rarr; the source file, its hash and its timestamp.</p>

    <p>Surface that in a Power BI report the client operates themselves and you've replaced a two-week reconstruction with a lookup. That report is the deliverable; the tables are just how it's fed.</p>

    <p><a href="demo-lineage.html" class="inline"><strong>Open the interactive demo &rarr;</strong></a> to see what that looks like &mdash; measure lineage, per-column quality across Bronze, Silver and Gold, blocking rules, load history and the PII register.</p>

    <h2>Where this stops</h2>

    <p>Being clear about the boundaries matters more than the implementation, and it's the part I'd want any engineer building this to say out loud to their stakeholders.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lineage starts at your ingestion boundary.</strong> You can evidence that a file arrived from a vendor system on a date with a given hash. What happened inside that platform &mdash; who edited a field at point of capture, and when &mdash; is their audit trail. If that matters, it's a vendor conversation, not an engineering one.</li>
      <li><strong>Only what flows through the platform is visible.</strong> Someone exporting to Excel and emailing it is outside this entirely, and in the organisations I work with that's common rather than exceptional.</li>
      <li><strong>This is evidence, not compliance.</strong> It demonstrates that the pipeline is traceable and access is controlled. It says nothing about whether consent was obtained, retention obligations were met, or the organisation's policies are adequate. Those are policy questions and they belong with someone qualified to answer them.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom means maintained.</strong> Purview is a product with a vendor. This is a framework you built. Documentation, version control and training are what make that a reasonable trade rather than a liability &mdash; and they have to actually be delivered.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention applies to the audit tables too.</strong> They accumulate indefinitely by default. Decide how long they're kept, and record that decision, or you've created a governance problem while solving one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why bother, rather than buying the catalogue</h2>

    <p>Two reasons, and the second is the one that decides it.</p>

    <p>The first is cost, which is the obvious one and the less interesting.</p>

    <p>The second: <strong>governance implemented as a separate system decays, and governance implemented as a by-product of the pipeline doesn't.</strong> A catalogue that depends on someone remembering to update it is accurate for about a quarter. An audit table that gets written because the notebook ran is accurate for as long as the platform runs. In organisations without a dedicated data function &mdash; which is most of them &mdash; that difference is the whole ballgame.</p>

    <p>It's also reusable. Built once, it's a component in every subsequent engagement, which for a solo consultancy is the closest thing to a product.</p>

    <p>If you've implemented something similar, I'd be interested in what you logged that I haven't &mdash; particularly anyone who's had this survive contact with an actual regulatory audit rather than an internal one.</p>

